<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Hartford (1638)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1638">1638</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Tripartite Treaty · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Hartford was signed in September 1638 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> colony and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohegan">Mohegan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansett</a> sachems after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a>. It declared the Pequot nation dissolved, divided the surviving captives between the Mohegan and the Narragansett, and forbade the use of the Pequot name. It confirmed English control of the Pequot lands of south-eastern Connecticut.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-hartford-1638.jpg" alt="Treaty of Hartford (1638)" width="320" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Haynes, John Hopkins, Edward Ludlow, Roger Miantonomo Uncas, 1590-1683 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Hartford_of_1638_(1743_copy).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
